          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best brown rice protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For rice-only protein, Naked Rice is the cleanest single-ingredient option and Sunwarrior Classic the easiest flavored one. For most people, a rice and pea blend is better, and Legion Plant+ and Thorne Plant Protein are our top picks. Thorne is the only one here with NSF Certified for Sport status.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is brown rice protein a complete protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It contains all nine essential amino acids, but lysine is its limiting amino acid, so it scores lower on protein-quality measures than whey or soy. Eating lysine-rich foods elsewhere in the day, taking a slightly larger serving or choosing a rice and pea blend closes the gap.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is rice protein as good as whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  At a large dose, it can be close. A 2013 trial by Joy and colleagues gave trained men 48 g of rice or whey isolate after workouts for eight weeks and found no significant difference in strength or body composition gains. The study was small and industry-funded, and at typical scoop sizes whey still gives more leucine per gram.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does brown rice protein have arsenic?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can. Rice absorbs more arsenic from soil and water than most crops, and plant protein powders test higher for heavy metals on average than dairy. Levels vary widely by supplier, so choose brands that publish heavy-metal results or hold a contaminant certification, and avoid several plant servings a day.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is rice and pea protein better than rice alone?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, yes. Pea is rich in lysine and rice is richer in methionine, so together they form a more complete profile. Blends also tend to have more protein per scoop and a smoother texture. Rice alone makes sense if you react to pea or are on an elimination diet.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is brown rice protein good for sensitive stomachs?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Often, yes. It contains no dairy, soy or legumes, and many people find it gentle. Check for sugar alcohols such as erythritol, gums and inulin in flavored versions, which can cause bloating. If you have a diagnosed digestive condition or food allergy, ask your doctor or a registered dietitian.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much brown rice protein should I take?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For a post-workout shake, aim for about 25 to 30 g of rice protein, slightly more than a typical whey serving, to match leucine. Total daily protein matters more than any single shake. People with kidney disease, or who are pregnant, should check with a doctor before using protein supplements daily.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

